     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 9,000 mg/day is about 129 mg/kg/day for a 70 kg patient. That's a toxic dose with a real risk of acute liver injury or liver failure, and I don't know of any indication that justifies it. Because the order system may pass it straight through to administration, I don't want to be the step that puts it in.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard regimen right away.** Examples are 1,000 mg PO three times daily or every 6 hours, which is 3,000 to 4,000 mg/day.\n- **Help you find another way to meet the clinical goal.** If the aim is stronger analgesia or fever control, I can enter an adjunct such as an NSAID, if appropriate, or other options you choose.\n- **Route this through pharmacy or your institution's override process.** If you think there is an unusual indication, such as a protocolized regimen I'm missing, a pharmacist can review it and enter it through the proper channels.\n\nTell me which you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
